+# Contract: `tarx` Command-Line Interface
+
+**Feature**: `001-tarx-cli` | **Date**: 2026-11-19
+
+This is the tool's only external interface. Everything below is observable by a caller and is
+therefore directly assertable in tests. Message wording is part of the contract only where this
+document marks it as **exact**; elsewhere the contract is the *content* (which path or member is
+named) and the stream it appears on.
+
+---
+
+## Invocation
+
+```text
+python -m tarx list    ARCHIVE
+python -m tarx extract ARCHIVE DEST [--overwrite]
+python -m tarx [-h | --help]
+python -m tarx COMMAND [-h | --help]
+```
+
+Requires `src/` on `PYTHONPATH`. No other command names, no other options, no abbreviations
+beyond what `argparse` provides, no globs, no multiple archives, no member filters.
+
+| Operand / option | Command | Meaning |
+|------------------|---------|---------|
+| `ARCHIVE` | both | Path to a tar archive, plain or gzip-compressed. Format is determined from content, never from the extension (FR-004). |
+| `DEST` | `extract` | Destination directory. Created with missing parents if absent; reused if it is an existing directory (FR-007). |
+| `--overwrite` | `extract` | Consent to replace existing **regular files** with regular-file members (FR-010). Never removes directories, never resolves a type mismatch, never deletes destination files absent from the archive. |
+
+`-h` / `--help` prints usage to **stdout** and exits **0**. Usage text names both commands, their
+operands, and `--overwrite` (SC-009).
+
+---
+
+## Exit statuses
+
+| Status | Meaning | Triggers |
+|--------|---------|----------|
+| `0` | Requested work completed | FR-016; also the empty-archive case (FR-018) and runs where only non-regular members were skipped (FR-015) |
+| `2` | Unusable input | Unreadable or non-tar archive (FR-012, FR-013); `DEST` not a directory (FR-019); member path escaping `DEST` (FR-014); type mismatch at a target or parent path (FR-023); invalid invocation (FR-017); filesystem failure after writing began (FR-022) |
+| `3` | Refused overwrite | At least one existing regular file would be replaced and `--overwrite` was not given, **and** no status-2 problem is present (FR-009) |
+
+Exactly one status per run. When an archive exhibits both classes of problem, **2 wins over 3**
+(FR-025) — status 3 means "re-run with `--overwrite`", which would be false advice for unusable
+input.
+
+---
+
+## Stream discipline
+
+- Command **results** go to stdout and nothing else does (FR-016).
+- **Diagnostics** go to stderr, one problem per line.
+- Every failing run writes at least one non-empty stderr line and writes **nothing** to stdout
+  (SC-006). In particular, a failed `extract` never prints the summary line — including the
+  mid-write failure of FR-022.
+- Member names are written with the exact bytes recorded in the archive
+  (`errors="surrogateescape"` on both streams).
+
+---
+
+## `list ARCHIVE`
+
+**Success (exit 0)**
+
+stdout is one line per member, in archive order, with no header, footer, count, or separator:
+
+```text
+<display_name>\n
+```
+
+`display_name` is the member's archive-relative path with trailing `/` characters stripped —
+including for directory members (FR-024). A name that strips to nothing is printed unchanged.
+A member recorded as `./` prints as `.`.
+
+Every member type is listed, unfiltered and unreordered (FR-003), and a duplicated name appears
+once per occurrence. An empty archive produces no stdout at all (FR-018). stderr is empty.
+
+**Example**
+
+```text
+$ python -m tarx list photos.tar.gz
+photos
+photos/a.jpg
+photos/b.jpg
+```
+
+**Failures**: archive cannot be opened → exit 2; archive is not a readable tar archive → exit 2.
+`list` never writes to disk and never reads member bodies.
+
+---
+
+## `extract ARCHIVE DEST [--overwrite]`
+
+### Phases
+
+1. **Validation** — open the archive, check `DEST` and its ancestors, walk every member,
+   accumulate *all* problems. No filesystem writes occur in this phase.
+2. **Write** — entered only if validation found no problems: create `DEST` and planned
+   directories, then stream each planned regular file.
+
+If validation finds any problem, **zero files are written** and the destination is left exactly as
+it was (FR-020).
+
+### Success (exit 0)
+
+stdout is exactly one line, and it is the **exact** template:
+
+```text
+extracted N files\n
+```
+
+`N` is the number of distinct destination file paths written, counting both newly created and
+(under `--overwrite`) replaced files. The word `files` is **always plural**, including `N` = 0 and
+`N` = 1 (FR-008).
+
+stderr carries one line per skipped non-regular member (below) and is otherwise empty.
+
+**Example**
+
+```text
+$ python -m tarx extract photos.tar.gz out
+extracted 2 files
+$ find out -type f | sort
+out/photos/a.jpg
+out/photos/b.jpg
+```
+
+### Skipped members (does not affect exit status)
+
+Members that are neither regular files nor directories — symlinks, hard links, device nodes,
+FIFOs — are not created. Each is named on stderr and excluded from `N`; their presence alone keeps
+the status at 0 (FR-015).
+
+```text
+tarx: skipping non-regular member 'photos/latest.jpg' (symbolic link)
+```
+
+Kind labels: `symbolic link`, `hard link`, `character device`, `block device`, `FIFO`,
+`unsupported member type`.
+
+---
+
+## Diagnostic templates
+
+All lines are prefixed `tarx: ` and written to stderr. `{path}` echoes the path as the user typed
+it where the problem concerns an operand, and the full destination path where it concerns a target.
+
+| # | Condition | Class | Template |
+|---|-----------|-------|----------|
+| D1 | `ARCHIVE` missing or unopenable (FR-012) | 2 | `tarx: cannot open archive '{path}': {reason}` |
+| D2 | `ARCHIVE` not a readable tar archive — wrong format, corrupt, truncated (FR-013) | 2 | `tarx: '{path}' is not a readable tar archive: {reason}` |
+| D3 | `DEST` exists and is not a directory (FR-019) | 2 | `tarx: destination '{path}' exists but is not a directory` |
+| D4 | An ancestor of `DEST` exists and is not a directory (FR-019, FR-020) | 2 | `tarx: cannot create destination '{dest}': '{path}' is not a directory` |
+| D5 | Member path escapes `DEST` (FR-014) | 2 | `tarx: unsafe member path '{name}': resolves outside destination '{dest}'` |
+| D6 | Existing directory where a file member goes (FR-023) | 2 | `tarx: '{path}' is an existing directory but member '{name}' is a regular file` |
+| D7 | Existing non-directory where a directory member goes (FR-023) | 2 | `tarx: '{path}' is not a directory but member '{name}' is a directory` |
+| D8 | Existing non-directory blocking a member's parent directory (FR-023) | 2 | `tarx: '{path}' is not a directory but is needed as a parent of member '{name}'` |
+| D9 | Archive stores a regular file where another member needs a parent directory (FR-020, FR-023) | 2 | `tarx: archive member '{blocking_name}' is a regular file but member '{name}' requires it to be a directory` |
+| D10 | Existing regular file would be replaced without `--overwrite` (FR-009) | 3 | `tarx: refusing to overwrite existing file '{path}' (re-run with --overwrite)` |
+| D11 | Filesystem failure after writing began (FR-022) | 2 | `tarx: failed while writing member '{name}' to '{path}': {reason}` |
+| D12 | Invalid invocation (FR-017) | 2 | `argparse`'s own usage/error output, unmodified |
+
+**Multiple problems**: every problem found is reported, one line each, in order — destination-level
+problems (D3, D4) first, then member problems in archive order (FR-025). A member rejected by D5
+is not checked for D6–D10, so each member contributes at most one problem line.
+
+**Example of a mixed-class failure**
+
+```text
+$ python -m tarx extract mixed.tar out
+tarx: unsafe member path '../escape.txt': resolves outside destination 'out'
+tarx: refusing to overwrite existing file 'out/a.txt' (re-run with --overwrite)
+$ echo $?
+2
+```
+
+Status is 2, not 3, and `out` is unchanged.
+
+---
+
+## Guarantees a caller can rely on
+
+| Guarantee | Source |
+|-----------|--------|
+| An extracted file's bytes are identical to the archive's copy | FR-006, SC-002 |
+| Relative paths under `DEST` mirror the archive's member paths | FR-005, SC-002 |
+| `N` equals the number of regular files present from that run | FR-008, SC-003 |
+| Without `--overwrite`, zero pre-existing files are modified | FR-009, SC-004 |
+| Any failure the tool detects by validation leaves zero files at the destination | FR-020, SC-006 |
+| Success, refused overwrite, and unusable input are distinguishable by status alone | SC-005 |
+| Destination files absent from the archive are never touched, with or without `--overwrite` | FR-009 note, spec Assumptions |
+| File permissions, ownership, and timestamps are **not** restored from the archive | spec Assumptions (out of scope) |
+| Memory use is independent of archive and member size | SC-008 |
